  "query_preview": "..\n\n  - Meteorological Overview:\nClosed upper level low off the coast of British Columbia and\nassociated upper level trough will dig south over the Pacific\nNorthwest by Saturday morning before tracking east along the\nCanadian border through the day on Sunday. Southwesterly flow\naloft ahead of the approaching upper level trough will continue to\ntransport Pacific moisture north and east over the Northern\nRockies through the timeframe. Stratiform rain and showers are the\npredominant precipitation drivers currently however isolated\nembedded and air mass thunderstorms could develop this afternoon\nas mid level stratiform dries out and daytime heating reaches its\nmax. High temperatures in the upper 70s to upper 80s today will\nfall to the 60s to low 70s by Sunday as the trough propagates\nthrough the CWA and exits Monday morning. Mid level winds will \nshift westerly by Sunday afternoon and create strong and gusty \nsurface winds for most of the CWA as the low tracks eastward. \nAreas along the northern Rocky Mountain Front will see the highest\nwinds due to pressure gradient forcing and cross-barrier \ndownsloping.\n\nSynoptic pattern next week will be primarily under the influence \nof longwave troughing over the Pacific Coast keeping southwesterly\nflow over the CWA bringing above normal PWAT's and warmer \ntemperatures until Friday when the long wave trough propagates \neastward. - Akins\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nEFI's indicate elevated winds above climate norms for the northern\nRocky Mountain Front as well as higher than normal QPF's for the\nSouthwest of Montana for Saturday and Sunday. NBM signals of 80%+\nprobabilities for wind gusts over 35 KT for Northern RMF and\nleeside of Crazy Mtns along Hwy 191. Confidence is lower but still\nworth mentioning for gusts over 50 KT with NBM signaling 30%\nprobability in the same areas. Probabilities could increase or\ndecrease depending on latitudinal position of the upper low's\ntrack as well as timing of the jet streak position during maximum\nmixed layer times on Sunday. For QPF's on Saturday, NBM is\nindicating a 40% probability for rainfall over 1\" for the West\nYellowstone area from noon Saturday until 6pm Sunday as well as\n30% probabilities of over 1\" in the Little Belts and Bridger Mtns.\n- Akins",
